  ---
  How this changes the design

  Scenario A: Small images, lenient API, local storage
  → Simple ThreadPool(20) + ProcessPool(cpu_count), done in minutes

  Scenario B: Large images, strict rate limit (5 req/s), S3 destination
  → Semaphore(5) + streaming downloads + chunked upload + DLQ for failures

  Scenario C: Real-time consumption, images arrive continuously
  → Async producer-consumer with asyncio, backpressure, and a message queue

  Scenario D: 50GB total payload, Lambda with 512MB RAM
  → Stream-process one image at a time, no batching in memory

  ---
  Interview framing

  ▎ "Before I design the solution, I'd want to understand the constraints because the architecture varies significantly. A 200MB
  ▎ batch on a beefy EC2 is a completely different problem than 50GB on a Lambda with a 5 req/s rate limit. The concurrency model,
  ▎ error handling, memory management, and even the choice of sync vs async all depend on these answers."

  This demonstrates you think like a data engineer (systems thinking, trade-offs, production-readiness) rather than just a coder who
  jumps to ThreadPoolExecutor.
